WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.813
Narrator: Good morning, Chad!

2
00:00:00.813 --> 00:00:04.153
Narrator: It's Friday, November twenty-seventh, and welcome back to your Daily Briefing.

3
00:00:04.153 --> 00:00:12.373
Narrator: Today: Drupal sites are under active attack with a federal deadline that lands today, Microsoft's big outage report is out, and Wall Street looks set to bounce in a short Black Friday session.

4
00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:13.622
Narrator: Let's start outside.

5
00:00:13.622 --> 00:00:17.798
Narrator: After the first freeze of the season on Thanksgiving morning, Fort Worth warms back up today.

6
00:00:17.798 --> 00:00:22.514
Narrator: Expect lows in the thirties this morning, sunshine, gusty winds, and afternoon highs back in the sixties.

7
00:00:22.514 --> 00:00:24.310
Narrator: The warming trend runs through Saturday.

8
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.801
Narrator: A dry cold front slides through Sunday, holding highs in the mid sixties with more sun and a breeze.

9
00:00:28.801 --> 00:00:36.525
Narrator: No watches or warnings for Tarrant County turned up in my check, but the National Weather Service page I pulled was a few days old, so give it a glance before you head out.

10
00:00:36.875 --> 00:00:39.326
Narrator: Around town, the Stockyards kick off the holidays today.

11
00:00:39.326 --> 00:00:46.765
Narrator: Cowboy Santa's Grand Arrival Parade rolls down East Exchange Avenue at eleven thirty this morning, and the tree lighting on the Livestock Exchange lawn is at six tonight.

12
00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:50.528
Narrator: The Fort Worth Symphony has "Home for the Holidays" at Bass Hall tonight and Saturday.

13
00:00:50.528 --> 00:00:53.416
Narrator: One practical note: the city shifted trash pickup for the holiday.

14
00:00:53.416 --> 00:01:00.023
Narrator: Thursday routes run today, Friday routes move to Saturday, and you can set out two extra bags beside your cart on your first pickup after Thanksgiving.

15
00:01:00.023 --> 00:01:04.005
Narrator: And City Council has a work session and a special called meeting on Monday, December first.

16
00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:05.967
Narrator: Now the national and world headlines.

17
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:06.533
Narrator: First, trade.

18
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:12.847
Narrator: The administration plans a new round of tariff hikes, reported at twenty percent, on China, the European Union, and about a dozen other partners.

19
00:01:12.847 --> 00:01:16.418
Narrator: Reports say they'd take effect after a comment period, in January at the earliest.

20
00:01:16.418 --> 00:01:18.073
Narrator: Markets are watching that one closely.

21
00:01:18.423 --> 00:01:21.103
Narrator: Second, Thanksgiving weather caused trouble on both coasts.

22
00:01:21.103 --> 00:01:27.100
Narrator: Storms, heavy rain, wind, and king tides hit coastal areas, with headlines putting about seventy million people under flood threats.

23
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.052
Narrator: The Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade was postponed until Saturday because of the weather.

24
00:01:31.402 --> 00:01:35.773
Narrator: Third, in southeast Iowa, the Army detonated five tons of lead azide near Middletown on Wednesday.

25
00:01:35.773 --> 00:01:40.814
Narrator: Residents were told to stay indoors, and the E.P.A. is monitoring the area while environmental testing continues.

26
00:01:40.814 --> 00:01:46.122
Narrator: And overseas, oil edged up Thursday as Middle East tensions persisted, with Brent crude around ninety dollars a barrel.

27
00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:46.968
Narrator: Okay, money.

28
00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:50.194
Narrator: Wednesday was the last full session before the holiday, and it was a wild one.

29
00:01:50.194 --> 00:01:58.632
Narrator: The Dow fell about eleven hundred points at its low, then clawed all the way back to close up one hundred sixty-four points, about a third of a percent, at roughly fifty thousand one hundred eighty-three.

30
00:01:58.632 --> 00:02:02.974
Narrator: The S and P five hundred slipped a quarter of a percent to about seven thousand one hundred eighty-three.

31
00:02:02.974 --> 00:02:06.903
Narrator: The Nasdaq fell six tenths of a percent to about twenty-three thousand five hundred eighty-six.

32
00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:08.482
Narrator: The story was the A.I. trade.

33
00:02:08.482 --> 00:02:11.194
Narrator: Doubts about A.I. profits and overspending hit chip stocks hard.

34
00:02:11.194 --> 00:02:16.617
Narrator: Nvidia fell almost four percent to about one hundred forty-nine dollars, after dropping as much as eight percent during the day.

35
00:02:16.617 --> 00:02:19.582
Narrator: Oracle, Micron, and SanDisk each lost four to five and a half percent.

36
00:02:19.582 --> 00:02:22.379
Narrator: Meanwhile homebuilders, materials, and transports led the gainers.

37
00:02:22.729 --> 00:02:23.553
Narrator: The data didn't help.

38
00:02:23.553 --> 00:02:29.720
Narrator: October core P.C.E. inflation came in at three point three percent year over year, a tick above forecasts, and headline P.C.E. hit three point eight percent.

39
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.233
Narrator: Third-quarter G.D.P. was revised up to four point three percent.

40
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:34.354
Narrator: The Fed's Beige Book flagged sustained cost pressures.

41
00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:40.049
Narrator: Put it together, and futures markets now price roughly a ninety-eight percent chance of a rate hike at the December eighth and ninth Fed meeting.

42
00:02:40.049 --> 00:02:40.521
Narrator: Yes, a hike.

43
00:02:40.871 --> 00:02:42.379
Narrator: This morning, futures are bouncing.

44
00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:47.379
Narrator: S and P futures were up close to two percent early, and Nasdaq one hundred futures were up more than two and a half.

45
00:02:47.379 --> 00:02:49.577
Narrator: Nvidia was up four to five percent before the open.

46
00:02:49.577 --> 00:02:52.465
Narrator: The ten-year Treasury yield was around four point nine two percent.

47
00:02:52.465 --> 00:02:59.060
Narrator: Gold rebounded too, up about two percent to roughly thirty-six hundred fifty dollars an ounce after hitting its lowest level in over a year on Wednesday.

48
00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.042
Narrator: Remember, stocks close early today at one p.m.

49
00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:02.421
Narrator: Eastern, which is noon our time.

50
00:03:02.421 --> 00:03:04.878
Narrator: The only data today are retail and wholesale inventories.

51
00:03:04.878 --> 00:03:09.878
Narrator: The big ones next week: jobs on Friday, December fourth, plus CrowdStrike and Okta earnings Tuesday after the close.

52
00:03:10.228 --> 00:03:11.156
Narrator: Now let's talk shop.

53
00:03:11.156 --> 00:03:17.236
Narrator: Microsoft published its final report on the November twelfth outage that took down Exchange Online for the better part of two days.

54
00:03:17.236 --> 00:03:23.223
Narrator: The root cause was a Windows defect in the configuration of Exchange's gateway servers, introduced by a code change back in July.

55
00:03:23.223 --> 00:03:27.493
Narrator: At its worst, more than eighty percent of North American gateway servers stopped responding.

56
00:03:27.493 --> 00:03:34.362
Narrator: Here's the uncomfortable part: Microsoft's load testing ran on older hardware with fewer cores, so it missed a bug that got worse on newer machines.

57
00:03:34.362 --> 00:03:39.375
Narrator: Microsoft says no queued enterprise mail was lost, after it stretched the retry window to forty-eight hours.

58
00:03:39.375 --> 00:03:46.940
Narrator: Internally, Microsoft declared a "Code Red," with M365 Core President Jason Henderson calling it the most customer-impacting incident in Exchange Online's history.

59
00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:50.792
Narrator: The company says it's aiming to resolve future worldwide incidents within one hour.

60
00:03:51.142 --> 00:03:57.061
Narrator: Why it matters to you: save the incident details from Service Health for your tenants now, because it only keeps thirty days of history.

61
00:03:57.061 --> 00:04:00.760
Narrator: And if a client took a real hit, look into whether they qualify for an S.L.A. credit.

62
00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.110
Narrator: Check the claim rules in Microsoft's own S.L.A. terms.

63
00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.378
Narrator: Two deadlines are worth your calendar too.

64
00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:08.987
Narrator: According to Microsoft's retirement schedule, basic authentication for S.M.T.P.

65
00:04:08.987 --> 00:04:14.788
Narrator: AUTH in Exchange Online gets disabled by default at the end of December, with full retirement for client submission in January.

66
00:04:14.788 --> 00:04:19.538
Narrator: That's every old scanner, copier, and line-of-business app that sends mail with a username and password.

67
00:04:19.538 --> 00:04:22.005
Narrator: Inventory them now and move them to O.Auth or a relay.

68
00:04:22.005 --> 00:04:25.202
Narrator: Then on February first, Entra retires built-in S.M.S. and voice M.F.A.

69
00:04:25.202 --> 00:04:29.587
Narrator: Start moving those users to Authenticator or passkeys over the holidays, while things are quiet.

70
00:04:29.937 --> 00:04:31.729
Narrator: Here's the one you'll want to act on today.

71
00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:34.355
Narrator: Drupal sites are under active attack, from two different flaws.

72
00:04:34.355 --> 00:04:37.063
Narrator: The first is a remote code execution bug in the Drupal installer.

73
00:04:37.063 --> 00:04:42.106
Narrator: CISA added it to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog, and the federal due date is today, November twenty-seventh.

74
00:04:42.106 --> 00:04:46.898
Narrator: The second, disclosed Wednesday, is a core access bypass that lets an attacker take over a site without logging in.

75
00:04:46.898 --> 00:04:50.898
Narrator: It hits sites left in an incomplete install state, and Drupal has confirmed attacks in the wild.

76
00:04:50.898 --> 00:04:56.191
Narrator: The fix is Drupal ten point six point twenty-three, eleven point three point twenty-three, or eleven point four point fourteen.

77
00:04:56.191 --> 00:05:01.066
Narrator: Run update dot P.H.P. after you upgrade, block the install script, and review activity on the user one admin account.

78
00:05:01.066 --> 00:05:04.150
Narrator: If the church site or any client site runs Drupal, that's your first move.

79
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:11.069
Narrator: Also on the KEV list: a VMware vCenter authentication bypass that a suspected China-linked group has exploited since early September.

80
00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:13.193
Narrator: Broadcom patched it on November nineteenth.

81
00:05:13.193 --> 00:05:19.071
Narrator: Two other items landed in that same batch, for the Optimizely platform and the Idunno AntiCsrf library, also due today.

82
00:05:19.421 --> 00:05:21.453
Narrator: For the MSP side of the house, two warnings.

83
00:05:21.453 --> 00:05:24.734
Narrator: A ConnectWise ScreenConnect flaw is being used against MSP technicians.

84
00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:26.813
Narrator: Patch it and kill active technician sessions.

85
00:05:26.813 --> 00:05:33.973
Narrator: And the N-able N-central exploit chain from earlier this month, nicknamed "4EverAble2Shell," has hit at least twenty-one servers, mostly for crypto-mining.

86
00:05:33.973 --> 00:05:38.686
Narrator: If you run on-prem N-central, keep the Legacy A.P.I. off and hunt for the indicators N-able published.

87
00:05:38.686 --> 00:05:45.847
Narrator: One breach note: 1Password says a leaked employee GitHub token let an attacker copy private source code and limited account metadata, but no vault secrets.

88
00:05:46.197 --> 00:05:46.677
Narrator: On to A.I.

89
00:05:46.677 --> 00:05:52.971
Narrator: OpenAI says it will merge ChatGPT and Codex usage into a single token-based quota for Plus, Pro, and Business starting in December.

90
00:05:52.971 --> 00:05:57.439
Narrator: Cognition released SWE two point one, a coding model built on Moonshot's open-weight Kimi K3.

91
00:05:57.439 --> 00:06:01.522
Narrator: For your local-model hobby, KRAFTON released Raon one mini under Apache two point oh.

92
00:06:01.522 --> 00:06:11.371
Narrator: It's a twenty-nine billion parameter mixture-of-experts model with about four billion active, and they say it can run a two hundred fifty-six thousand token context on twenty gigabytes of V.RAM with NVFP4.

93
00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:15.887
Narrator: And Z.ai says GLM five point five is still on track for December, denying a report of a delay.

94
00:06:16.237 --> 00:06:17.199
Narrator: Big tech quick hits.

95
00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:24.707
Narrator: Nvidia, with Jensen Huang reinstated as C.E.O. on Monday, has halted A.I. server shipments to Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia, tied to new U.S.

96
00:06:24.707 --> 00:06:25.959
Narrator: Commerce export licensing.

97
00:06:25.959 --> 00:06:30.290
Narrator: S and P Global Ratings warned it could downgrade Nvidia's credit over governance concerns.

98
00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:35.343
Narrator: Anthropic, which started trading publicly last week, is still below its eighty-three dollar I.P.O. price.

99
00:06:35.343 --> 00:06:40.060
Narrator: And SAP is reportedly preparing to cut at least ten thousand jobs in an A.I.-driven restructuring.

100
00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.182
Narrator: Alright, let's recap your action items.

101
00:06:42.182 --> 00:06:46.407
Narrator: One: patch any Drupal sites today to the latest core release, and check the user one account.

102
00:06:46.407 --> 00:06:49.269
Narrator: Two: patch vCenter and ScreenConnect if you have them anywhere.

103
00:06:49.269 --> 00:06:54.267
Narrator: Three: export the outage records from Service Health before they age out, and check S.L.A. credit eligibility.

104
00:06:54.267 --> 00:06:55.403
Narrator: Four: start your S.M.T.P.

105
00:06:55.403 --> 00:06:57.629
Narrator: AUTH inventory before the end-of-December cutoff.

106
00:06:57.629 --> 00:07:00.991
Narrator: And five: plan the move off S.M.S. and voice M.F.A. before February first.

107
00:07:01.341 --> 00:07:02.252
Narrator: That's your briefing.

108
00:07:02.252 --> 00:07:06.636
Narrator: Enjoy the sunshine and the leftovers, maybe skip the Black Friday crowds, and have a great day, Chad.

109
00:07:06.636 --> 00:07:07.591
Narrator: Talk to you next time!
